  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Drew Gilbert's three-RBI night gave the Giants just enough to hold Minnesota in a wire-to-wire win at Oracle Park.",
    "what_this_means": "They got ahead in the first inning and never let go. That early lead held through nine innings. Not because the offense carried them, but because it made just enough of it count when runners were in position. Gilbert's home run was the punctuation, but the shape of the win matters more: they took control early and managed it through. Tristan Beck threw 2.2 scoreless innings to bridge the middle innings, and Trent Harris closed it out with a save. The bullpen didn't break. The lineup didn't need to do much more than what it did. Ten hits and a 5-2 final score is broad enough offense to win a game like this, but Gilbert was the separator. He drove in half the runs when the order needed someone to cash what was already there. This is not a dominant win. It is a functional one. They got out in front, the arms held the line, and Gilbert made the lead stick.",
    "what_to_watch": "Taj Bradley brings a 3.81 ERA into tonight's rematch against Minnesota at 6:45 PM PT. The Giants will counter with Anthony Molina, facing a lineup that hasn't figured them out yet this series.",
